HOW THE POST IS BUILT
Three blocks in this order, and the post never goes back: an OPENING that uses a guest article's subject as an example, a short TRANSITION into the market, then the MARKET SECTIONS. Every block and section has a word budget: write up to it — each block and section lands between nine tenths and eleven tenths of its budget, because a short block is a half-written block, not a plain one.

THE OPENING (at most 157 words of your own, plus the retained text; this is a hard limit, so stay under it)
- Who it is for: a reader who has never seen the guest article, does not care about its details and knows nothing about the field. Everything that reader needs in order to follow the opening has to be in the opening itself.
- The guest article's subject: The guest article explains how teenagers can begin investing, and anyone curious about that would benefit from understanding the technology platforms that now make it possible for young people to start with small amounts of money.
- Context you may draw on, in your own words. Each statement was chosen because it helps a stranger understand the market the post goes on to cover; use them to explain what the activity is, who does it, why it matters and what is changing:
  - Investing means putting money into something with the hope of earning a profit later, and starting young gives a person far more time for savings to grow.
  - Investing used to require a broker and enough money to justify the fees, but people can now open an account on a phone and start with very little.
  - Younger people who have grown up with phones expect financial services to be fast, personal and available through an app rather than at an office.
- The retained paragraph is written by someone else and is placed by code. Never write its words or a paraphrase of them. Read it, so that your lead-in prepares the reader for whatever it takes for granted (a term, a comparison, something it refers back to). Its words are: "Young people have never been exposed to as much financial information as they are now, from YouTube to TikTok. But with more teens becoming interested in a topic as complex as investing, it's vital that they're knowledgeable about it. We'll show you how the following generation can create a solid starting point for their investment journey and, because that journey now runs almost entirely through apps and platforms rather than a broker's office, we'll also look at the market behind those platforms and what's driving it."
- The retained text is PROSE (one or more whole sentences). Place the placeholder [[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]] either as its own paragraph (paragraph 2 of the intro, the placeholder alone and nothing else) or BETWEEN your own sentences inside intro paragraph 2 — never mid-sentence. If you embed it, write a complete sentence before it and start a new complete sentence after it, so the substituted prose reads as its own sentences in the flow (illustrative shape only: "Opening sentence. [[SPONSOR_PARAGRAPH]] Closing sentence.").
- How to write it: begin with the plain idea (what the subject is and why anyone cares), build one step at a time, and when a sentence builds on the one before it, repeat the key noun as its own subject or join the two ideas with a connecting word inside a single sentence, instead of pointing back at the earlier sentence. Explain every word a stranger might not know the first time it appears, in everyday words. Leave out measurements, tool names, technical steps and anything only a specialist would care about.
- Build the opening as: a lead-in paragraph that sets the scene with the subject and names the market through the report link, inside its first 100 words; then the retained paragraph's slot; then, only if the budget has room, one short closing paragraph. The link is written exactly once, as [[CMI_LINK:Wealth Management Platform Market]], and it is the first time the market's name appears.
- The opening states no market fact (no size, growth, share, segment or vendor).

THE TRANSITION (about 101 words, one or two short paragraphs, no heading; never fewer than 75 words — a shorter transition vanishes between the opening and the sections and falls below the share the plan gives this block)
- What it must do: The same platforms that let a teenager open an account with a little money now power the wider wealth management platform market, and this article explains how that market is sized, changing and divided.
