QUESTION 2 (Q3): What are the latest trends in wealth management platforms?
Facts this answer may use:
- Rapid digitization and a surge in online trading activities boost sales of wealth management platforms.
- Growing adoption of robo-advisory services and virtual financial assistance solutions lets end users manage their wealth more efficiently and drives revenue growth.
- Increasing competition among wealth management firms fuels demand for versatile wealth management platforms.
- Changing needs and expectations of younger client segments push wealth advisory firms to adopt comprehensive platforms.
- Edward Jones is rolling out a hybrid digital investment advice offering to make its wealth management platform more appealing to younger investors. (source: MSN, 2026-08)
- Edward Jones launched its Digital Managed Solutions robo advisor to target young earners and savers just starting to invest. (source: Financial Planning, 2026-08)
    detail: The service requires only $5,000 to get automated investing backed by human advisors, versus Fidelity's $25,000 and Vanguard's $50,000 minimums for hybrid services; Edward Jones is approaching young i
- Apex Fintech Solutions formed an alliance with FusionIQ, a provider of cloud-based digital wealth management technology, to give institutions a faster route to launching modern investing experiences. (source: Yahoo Finance, 2026-09)
    detail: The alliance combines Apex's clearing, custody and brokerage infrastructure with FusionIQ's end-to-end digital wealth platform, intended to reduce integration complexity and shorten time-to-market for
- Vanguard agreed to acquire Altruist, a digital wealth management platform and custodian for independent financial advisors, in a deal valuing Altruist at approximately $4 billion. (source: fintechfutures, 2026-08)
- Broadridge Financial Solutions expanded its digital assets platform to U.S. wealth management firms, giving broker-dealers, RIAs and wealth managers a single environment for cryptocurrencies and tokenized securities alongside conventional investments. (source: InvestmentNews, 2026-09)
    detail: Tom Carey, Broadridge's Global Head of Product and Technology, said digital assets are moving from the margins to mainstream investment conversations; approximately 32% of financial advisors invested 
- Field launched as a wealth management technology company building foundational infrastructure for the advisory industry, led by former Envestnet CEO Bill Crager. (source: Pulse 2.0, 2026-05)
    detail: The launch was supported by two acquisitions through iAltA: BridgeFT, a wealthtech API platform providing multi-custodian data connectivity and normalization, and Precept, whose technology connects da
- Sixty-three percent of RIAs are currently using AI, double the share in 2024, but only one in ten firms uses the technology as a fully integrated part of its business strategy, according to advisor surveys cited at RIA Edge Orange County. (source: Wealth Management.com, 2026-10)
    detail: The figure was cited by Ashley Longabaugh, global head of wealth management at consultancy Celent; Tim Foley of AdvisorEngine and Taylor Ciavarra of Advisor360 said firms should tie AI adoption to cor
- The wealth management industry is entering a new phase after years of heavy investment in digital channels, robo-advice and advisor workstations. (source: Finextra, 2026-07)
- Leading digital wealth platforms are used each month by 9% of Singapore's internet users, nearly three times the share in the next-ranked market, Malaysia, according to the e-Conomy SEA 2026 report by Google, Temasek and Bain & Company. (source: Fintech News Singapore, 2026-10)
- CircleBlack appointed Vikram Chugh, who built the wealth management platform at Robertson Stephens, as its new CEO. (source: Wealth Management.com, 2026-10)
